  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Show that there are two possible triangles $\\triangle ABC$ for which $a=6$, $c=9$ and $\\angle A=30^\\circ$. Find the two possible measures of $\\angle C$, correct to the nearest degree.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\angle C=49^\\circ$ or $131^\\circ$.\n\nThe sine rule gives supplementary angles, and both leave a positive third angle.",
      "worked": "Here $c\\sin A=9\\sin30^\\circ=4.5$, so $c\\sin A<a<c$. Hence two triangles are possible.\n\nUsing the sine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\sin C}{9}&=\\frac{\\sin30^\\circ}{6}\\\\\n\\sin C&=\\frac{9\\sin30^\\circ}{6}\\\\\n\\sin C&=\\frac34\\\\\nC_1&=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac34\\right)\\\\\nC_1&\\approx48.59^\\circ\\\\\nC_2&=180^\\circ-\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac34\\right)\\\\\nC_2&\\approx131.41^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values are less than $150^\\circ$, so both leave a positive angle $B=180^\\circ-30^\\circ-C$.\n\nTherefore $\\angle C=49^\\circ$ or $131^\\circ$, to the nearest degree.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
